{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nParent epic `a4bd-4ca8-c44f-49bb` (despotic-floriated-leopard). `managed_refs`\n(`src/rebar/reducer/_managed_refs.py`) marks a ref managed the instant it is created LOCALLY and\nis strictly monotonic, so guard G3 in the inbound removal path cannot tell a brand-new\nnever-pushed local link from a peer-deleted one. This story adds the missing evidence store; it\nchanges NO removal behavior (S3 does that).\n\nDirection is fixed by the epic: retain durable association/provenance, never treat an unqualified\nmissing link as deletion evidence. The record is a FULL record (vendor link id, direction,\nconfirming pass, confirmed-at), not a boolean — see the epic's \"Chosen direction\" section and the\nepic comment \"Decomposition design calls\" (D1 storage home, D2 key shape, D3 pass_id).\n\n## Scope\n\nNew module `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/peer_confirmations.py`, modelled on the sidecar\n`impossible_links.py` (SCHEMA_VERSION constant, store-relative path, atomic mkstemp +\n`os.replace` save, tolerant load).\n\n- Store file: `.tickets-tracker/.bridge_state/peer_confirmations.json`.\n- Record key: `(source_local_id, target_local_id, relation)` — LOCAL ids, relation-scoped,\n  matching `_inbound_unlink_one` (relation-scoped since e39f) and `impossible_links.record_key`.\n- Record fields: `link_id`, `direction` (`outbound` | `snapshot`), `confirmed_pass`,\n  `confirmed_at` (epoch ns), `source` (`push` | `snapshot` | `backfill`).\n- API: `open_store(repo_root)`, `is_confirmed(source, target, relation)`,\n  `record(source, target, relation, *, link_id, direction, pass_id, source_kind)`, `save()`.\n\n**Write seam (three edits, callback-shaped).** The success point is\n`dispatch_one.py::_update_one_dispatch_links` at the `set_relationship` call, but that function\nhas neither a binding store nor a pass id and must not gain knowledge of either:\n\n1. `dispatch_one.py` — `update_one(...)` and `_update_one_dispatch_links(...)` each gain an\n   optional keyword `link_confirm: Callable[..., None] | None = None`; the sole internal call\n   site (`dispatch_one.py:454`) forwards it. `None` preserves today's behavior byte-for-byte.\n   After `set_relationship` returns, invoke `link_confirm(to_key=..., relation=..., link_id=...)`.\n   The call is inside the existing `try`, AFTER `_links_applied += 1`, so a failed\n   `set_relationship` records nothing. The invocation is itself wrapped so that ANY exception\n   from the callback (`record()` / `save()`) is logged to stderr and swallowed — a confirmation\n   write must never fail an outbound link that already landed, and must never perturb the\n   counters.\n2. `dispatch_one.py` — capture `set_relationship`'s return value (typed `dict[str, Any]` in\n   `_backend.py::SupportsLinks`, currently discarded) and pass `result.get(\"id\")` as `link_id`\n   when the return is a Mapping, else `None`. Backends may legitimately return no id; a missing\n   id is recorded as `null` and is NOT a failure.\n3. `apply_handlers.py::handle_update` — build the callback and pass it into the `update_one(...)`\n   call at line 211. This is the PRODUCTION outbound-update path, and its `BatchApplyContext`\n   already holds `client`, `repo_root`, `pass_id` and `binding_store`, so no new plumbing is\n   needed anywhere (the same context-to-dispatch pattern `synced_fields` already uses). The\n   callback translates keys to the record shape: source is `mutation[\"local_id\"]`, target is\n   `ctx.binding_store.get_local_id(to_key)`. **When the reverse map returns None the target is\n   unbound — skip recording entirely** (never persist a Jira key in a local-id field); the\n   confirmation is picked up next pass, or by S2 from a snapshot. `apply_outbound.py`'s typed\n   leaf is deliberately NOT edited: it delegates to the same `update_one` and passing no\n   callback leaves it exactly as today.\n\n**Durability — three edits, not one.** Adding the path to `_rel_files` alone is insufficient:\n`reconcile_helpers.py::_commit_binding_store_snapshot` builds a SECOND, parallel\n`_tracked_basenames` set (the bug-1e08 per-file idempotency guard) and returns \"already\nup-to-date\" when no tracked basename is staged — so a peer-confirmation-only change would be\nsilently dropped.\n\n1. `git_adapter.py` — add `PEER_CONFIRMATIONS_FILE = f\"{BRIDGE_STATE_DIR}/peer_confirmations.json\"`\n   and export it in `__all__`.\n2. `reconcile_helpers.py` — append it to `_rel_files`.\n3. `reconcile_helpers.py` — append its basename to `_tracked_basenames`.\n\nLoad/save is fail-OPEN (like `bindings-retired.json`, NOT fail-closed like `bindings.json`): a\ncorrupt or absent store degrades to \"nothing confirmed\" without aborting the pass. S4 supplies\nthe backfill that makes \"nothing confirmed\" safe.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 `peer_confirmations.py` has a `SCHEMA_VERSION` constant and is under 400 LOC; every\n      edited module stays under the 800-line cap. Proof: `bash -c 'git archive HEAD | tar -xO\n      src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_one.py | wc -l'` per edited file, each < 800.\n- [ ] AC2 `record()` then `is_confirmed()` is True for exactly that `(source, target, relation)`\n      and False for the same pair under a different relation. Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_store.py -k relation_scoped`.\n- [ ] AC3 A successful outbound ADD through `_update_one_dispatch_links` invokes `link_confirm`\n      and writes a record with `direction=\"outbound\"`, `source=\"push\"`, the dispatched relation\n      and the `pass_id`; a `set_relationship` that raises writes NO record. Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_store.py -k dispatch`.\n- [ ] AC4 `link_id` is taken from `set_relationship`'s returned mapping (`result[\"id\"]`) and is\n      `null` when the backend returns no id or a non-mapping — neither case is an error. Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_store.py -k link_id`.\n- [ ] AC5 An unbound target (`binding_store.get_local_id(to_key)` returns None) records NOTHING\n      and does not raise. Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_store.py -k unbound`.\n- [ ] AC6 With `link_confirm=None`, `_links_computed` / `_links_applied` are identical to\n      pre-change behavior on the success, failure and dedup-skip paths. Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_store.py -k counter`.\n- [ ] AC7 The store survives a ticket-log SNAPSHOT compaction (confirm, compact, re-open, still\n      confirmed). Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_store.py -k compaction`.\n- [ ] AC8 A corrupt or absent `peer_confirmations.json` yields an empty store and does NOT raise.\n      Proof: `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_store.py -k corrupt`.\n- [ ] AC9 `_commit_binding_store_snapshot` COMMITS when `peer_confirmations.json` is the ONLY\n      changed file (proves both `_rel_files` and `_tracked_basenames` were updated). Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_store.py -k commit_only`.\n- [ ] AC10 A `link_confirm` callback that RAISES is logged and swallowed: the outbound link\n      still counts as applied and `_links_applied` is unchanged. Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_store.py -k callback_raises`.\n- [ ] AC11 Gates green. Proof: `make lint` exit 0, `make typecheck` exit 0,\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler` exit 0.\n\n## Testing\n\n- New suite `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_store.py` covering store\n  round-trip, relation scoping, corrupt/absent load, compaction survival, the dispatch write\n  point (success, raise, dedup-skip), `link_id` extraction, the unbound-target skip, a raising\n  callback, counter parity under `link_confirm=None`, and the commit-when-only-changed-file case.\n- Regression: `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler` stays green — this story is additive and must\n  not alter existing dispatch or removal behavior.\n- Full command set: `make lint && make typecheck && pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler`.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nSnapshot-sourced confirmation (S2), the removal decline (S3), backfill (S4)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786559823820929001,"uuid":"4f7183b8-a7dd-452a-846c-00b19c85a751"}